// @check
// @error {[string]: i32}[] isn't equatable (a field is not value-comparable)
// @error `eqL` compares its type parameter with `==` here: {[string]: i32}[] isn't equatable (a field is not value-comparable) — compare a projection whose components are value-comparable, and only one that no two distinct values share
// @error {[string]: i32} | null isn't equatable — a map has no defined value equality
// @error `eqN` compares its type parameter with `==` here: {[string]: i32} | null isn't equatable — a map has no defined value equality
// GRADUATED from `silent-class-inventory` D421 — D35's and D44's shape ONE CONSTRUCTOR UP.
//
// D35 closed "a `needle: T` the checker will not `==` loses that refusal in the instantiation"
// and D44 closed the same thing for `+`. Both grids varied the delivery of the needle, the
// receiver and the callee, and in every cell the operand's TYPE was `T` itself. `noteBinCstr`
// records a deferred constraint only when an operand's type IS a hole, so `a: T[]` — a
// `TyArray` OVER a hole — recorded nothing, the pin had nothing to re-ask, and every gate in
// the body answered OPAQUE about the instance.
//
// EVERY REFUSAL BELOW IS THE DIRECT SPELLING'S OWN, and the pair is what makes that checkable:
// each `…Direct` is the comparison written out and each `…Pinned` is the identical comparison
// reached through a type parameter one constructor down. Before D421 the `Direct` half was a
// loud check reject and the `Pinned` half was `vl check` rc 0 over a module the engine refuses.
//
// ── THE `+` HALF WENT IN D721 AND THE `==` HALF HAS NOW GONE THE SAME WAY (D751/D752). ──
// This fixture used to hold six `+` functions and ten `==` ones. The `+` six went first: a
// concat inspects no element, so the element axis never decided it. The `==` ten followed for
// a DIFFERENT reason and it is worth keeping the distinction — a compare DOES inspect its
// element, so each rep needed a core rather than one lowering; `emitListEqGCore` is that core,
// parameterised on the (wrapper, backing) pair plus an element rung. The struct list, the
// `f64[]`, the `F[]`, the nested `Circle[][]` and the `Circle[] | null` niche all RUN now and
// are read back BY VALUE in the sibling `eq-concat-type-param-under-constructor-runs.vl`.
//
// D421'S ARGUMENT SURVIVES BOTH MOVES INTACT, which is the reason to say this here rather
// than delete the paragraph: the row is about a refusal LOST IN THE INSTANTIATION, and what
// it needs is that the two spellings AGREE. They still do — the maps below refuse at both,
// everything graduated runs at both — and `addL`/`eqL` are kept in the module so the pin
// still has `T[]`-shaped `+` and `==` constraints to record.
//
// WHAT IS LEFT IS A DESIGN RULING, NOT A MISSING CORE. A map has no defined value equality
// (its entries are insertion-ordered and observable that way), so `==` over one is refused off
// the `unsupported-lowering` channel entirely. `silent-class-inventory` D754.

type CM = {[string]: i32}
function eqL<T>(a: T[], b: T[]): boolean { a == b }
function addL<T>(a: T[], b: T[]): T[] { a + b }
function eqN<T>(a: T | null, b: T | null): boolean { a == b }

function mkm(): CM { const m: CM = Map(); m["k"] = 1; m }

// ── `==` at an element rep with no list-compare core ──────────────────────────────────
function mapListDirect(a: CM[], b: CM[]): boolean { a == b }
function mapListPinned(a: CM[], b: CM[]): boolean { eqL(a, b) }

// ── THE OTHER CONSTRUCTOR: `T | null`. A nullable whose inner has no compare core is the
//    same lost refusal, and the `Circle | null` cell that DOES have one is in the sibling
//    fixture because its correct end state is `runs`. ──────────────────────────────────
function nulMapDirect(a: CM | null, b: CM | null): boolean { a == b }
function nulMapPinned(a: CM | null, b: CM | null): boolean { eqN(a, b) }

print(mapListDirect([mkm()], [mkm()]))
print(mapListPinned([mkm()], [mkm()]))
print(addL([1], [2]).length)
print(nulMapDirect(mkm(), null))
print(nulMapPinned(mkm(), null))
